      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy, didn't I? Sorry about that. Let's get back to the science.\n\nBone meal is a slow-release fertilizer that contains phosphorus, nitrogen, and other micronutrients. The phosphorus in bone meal is typically in the form of calcium phosphate, which is a relatively stable compound that doesn't readily react with the soil pH. This is in contrast to blood meal, which is high in nitrogen and can be more pH-sensitive.\n\nWhen bone meal is added to the soil, the calcium phosphate is slowly released as the soil microorganisms break it down. This process is relatively pH-independent, meaning that the phosphorus is released at a relatively consistent rate regardless of the soil pH. This makes bone meal a good choice for soils with a pH as high as 6.2, like yours.\n\nIn contrast, blood meal is high in nitrogen, which can be more pH-sensitive. When blood meal is added to the soil, the nitrogen can be released more quickly, but this can also lead to a more rapid pH increase. This might be problematic if you're already at a pH of 6.2, as it could lead to an overgrowth of alkaliphilic microorganisms that",
